resultText:
**Function:** `createTasks` in `src/domain/tasks.ts:294-337`.

**Root cause:** `createTasks` runs in two passes. Pass 1 (lines 314–324) creates every spec's task and, if the spec carries a `temp_id`, immediately records `tempIdToTaskId.set(temp_id, task.id)` — mapping the client's temporary id to the *real* id just assigned to that same spec. Pass 2 (lines 326–331) then walks each spec's `depends_on` list and resolves each reference via `tempIdToTaskId.get(ref) ?? ref` before calling `addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, dependsOnTaskId)`.

**The triggering input shape:** a single spec in the batch whose own `depends_on` array contains that same spec's own `temp_id` string. For example:

```json
{
  "specs": [
    { "temp_id": "T1", "title": "...", "depends_on": ["T1"], ... }
  ]
}
```

No spec ever literally repeats its real (DB-assigned) task id in its own `depends_on` — that id doesn't even exist yet when the client writes the request. But by the time pass 2 runs, `tempIdToTaskId.get("T1")` resolves to that same spec's newly-created real `task.id`, so the call becomes `addDependency(db, results[i].task_id, results[i].task_id)` — identical ids on both sides — tripping `addDependency`'s "A task cannot depend on itself" check even though the client's JSON never mentions the real id twice.

A variant of the same bug: two specs whose `temp_id`s cross-reference in a way that resolves back to the same spec isn't required — the minimal trigger is just a spec listing its *own* `temp_id` in its own `depends_on` array.
